Robin Hanson:
> I thought the whole point of the decoherence literature was to find
> objective ways (not equi-norm) to define worlds, to choose the basis.
> I was assuming one of these approaches would be successful, and that
> thermodynamics would ensure a finite basis.  Hence I explicitly
> assumed objective and finite splits in my first post.
>
You're right, the decoherence approach does remove most the ambiguity
in defining worlds and bases, but my doubts still remain about the
"counting paradox", because you're ignoring the weighting that each
world comes supplied with.  If we have:

   |psi> = |1>*1 + |2>*3                (ignoring normalision)
         = |1> + |2> + |2> + |2>

then why not count world2 three times as often as world1?

