From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Thu, 20 Oct 1994 12:16:27 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-358] Challenge to uploaders

John Clark, replying to my expressed doubts that a talking apple would be
subjectively aware, wrote:	
>An entirely reasonable attitude, after all ,extraordinary claims
>require extraordinary evidence ; however, suppose we lived in a
>world in which apples were known throughout history as
>intellectual powerhouses, they have come up with important
>scientific theories, profound philosophy and moving love poetry.
>In such a world I would conclude that the physical structure of
>humans is not the only way consciousness can develop.  
	
Yes, extraordinary evidence indeed, and a reasonable conclusion.  But the
physical structure of the apple is such that there would be no credible
theory for a physical representation of concepts, memory, and data
processing.  Therefore the only possible explanation would be that the
apples had souls which were capable of vibrating air molecules in the
required manner, and in this extraordinary world, substance dualism would
be the preferred theory of mind.   

So, keep Turing as an important test, if the test is passed by a structure
physically resembling the human brain our confidence rating is high, as
the structure starts to differ whilst still allowing some plausible
materialist theory of mind we lower our confidence rating slightly (or
demand a more lengthy interrogation), and when testing a subject with an
apple-like structure demand extraordinary evidence.  It would be much more
reasonable to assign subjective awareness to a "Chinese Room" than an apple.
 
John, replying to Ed Rgis, wrote:
>Similarity in behavior is a MUCH better test of consciousness
>than similarity of physical structure. Consider a man awake and
>a hour latter asleep. One brain is conscious one is not, the
>behavior of the man is radically different in the two cases,

We should check for eye movements.  If the subject was in REM sleep
he would be conscious of his dreaming whilst exhibiting very different
behaviour compared to his waking time.  I don't believe there is evidence
that we have some awareness throughout the whole of sleeping time, but I'd
sooner rule out awareness in talking rocks or apples than in a healthy man
apparently in non-REM sleep.
 
-- 
