>examine my point of view for a flaw, however.) My attitude is something

I'm curious to find out if you discover any flaws in that point of view--
one possibility is that if you treat yourself as a object in that sense
for too long at a time, you could be making improvements that you 
actually don't care about. 

>like: "I feel good about myself because of what I make of myself, and how I
>deal with challenges to my being."  I suppose I am more of a Nietzchean
>egoist. A more radical individualist might say something like, "I feel good
>about myself  because I am me", and be unconcerned with self-transformation
>or personal power.

I may be an intermediate case--my attitude is some mix of "I feel good
about myself because I like being me" and "I'm loyal to myself because
(for no reason that goes into words at the moment) there's something
valuable about me and I will *not* throw myself away". 

I put effort into self-improvement, but a lot of it seems to be
that I'm haunted by a sense of how I should feel--for example, I
tend to have difficulty getting myself to take any action, and
that difficulty just *feels* bad, in rather a Nietzchian* sense.

I've been having less trouble with it lately, and I'm not sure
which changes have helped the most. (When I have a problem, I
tend to throw a whole bunch of different strategies at it, without
pursuing any of them very hard, and checking each of them for
feel. It may seem weird, but I'm not likely to hurt myself, and
it's more likely to keep me interested than being gung ho about
one thing--and it sometimes works.)

The two most likely candidates for what helped are that my refusing
to put up with waves of self-hatred is finally paying off, and that
I'm getting better at Alexander Technique--a way of moving more
easily which addresses "mental" habits as well as "physical" ones. 

