From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Thu, 20 Oct 1994 09:09:58 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-362] Marketing AI

In message [#94-10-264] sasha1@netcom.com (Alexander Chislenko)
writes about the safety-paranoid society preventing the AI
development:

FH> Right now we build machines for specific ends and we insist
    they do their job exactly as specified, no more, no less.
    Nobody would design, nobody would market, and nobody would
    buy a machine of any kind that threatened to break out of
    control. 

AC> ... this may be a greater obstacle to the appearance of AI
    than any theoretical and hardware hurdles. Sure, you can make
    *some* progress in the closet after the paranoid bureaucrats
    stop your public experiments by burdening you with zillions
    of complicated and contradicting requirements and
    restrictions...  However, AI ... needs open research and
    mass-market testing, connection to high-speed networks, and
    lots of other things that are impossible to hide from
    retrograds-in-power.

The problem here seems much deeper to me than just
retrograds-in-power, though that's part of it.  It is very hard
to imagine anybody even wanting to *buy* a machine that
spontaneously, unpredictably, and autonomously modified its
behavior in any deep sense.  Of course if you are talking about a
chess computer that just learns as it goes, then my objection
doesn't hold.  Clearly there is a market for that.  But the chess
computer market is not looking for a machine that might someday
decide that enpassant was an illogical move and unilaterally ban
it, or that Go was the only serious board game, or that refused
to play out a position because it was boring, or that mocked your
play or pushed pawns when your back was turned or that tried to
make you nervous by insulting your sex life or refused to let you
take a move back.  I can add to this list all day long, as can
you.

Now we're just talking about a chess computer here.  Take this
analysis and apply it to automobiles or operating systems and you
really have a terrible marketing problem, it seems to me.

